ec\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:302:pose action <open|list|show|statement|resolve|cancel|waive|invalidate> ...  # abre, lê e resolve pedidos materiais a uma pessoa ou a um sistema externo\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:328:pose report-limitation --title \"...\" --kind limitation|bug|suggestion [--body \"...\"] [--submit]\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:413:- **Governança de docs** (spec `pose-docs-governance-contract`) — contrato opt-in por projeto para documentação, mesma mecânica do assessment de capability acima: ausente, todo projeto segue válido em todo lugar. `pose docs-init [--profile library|service|cli|monorepo]` cria um manifesto declarando as raízes governadas de documentação e, por doc, `path`/`doc_type` (Diátaxis `tutorial`/`howto`/`reference`/`explanation`, ou valor customizado)/`topics`/`owns`/`applies_to`/opcionalmente `review_after`. `pose docs-check [--json] [--explain <rule>]` roda sete regras determinísticas e offline — doc declarado ausente do disco, arquivo presente mas não declarado, frontmatter mínimo faltando (`title`/`doc_type`), link relativo quebrado, referência tipada quebrada (`spec:`/`adr:`/`knowledge:`/`doc:`/...), staleness (data de review da própria entrada, ou a janela default do manifesto contada a partir do último commit que a tocou) e uma varredura de segurança reusando a mesma checagem determinística de instrução insegura/forma de segredo que as skills já rodam — cada uma com severidade configurável (`error`/`warning`/`off`). `pose check --strict` incorpora o `docs-check` quando o manifesto existe (opt-in por presença); apenas erros bloqueiam, avisos aparecem sem bloquear. `pose docs-sync` sincroniza metadados de governança. Ferramenta MCP: `pose_docs_state`. O project-state ganha uma seção Docs aditiva (presença/perfil/raízes do manifesto, contagens de declarados/não declarados/stale/erro/aviso).\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:566:     - `pose report-limitation --title \"...\" --kind limitation|bug|suggestion [--submit]`\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:951:**Checks hospedados e o memo residente do Git.** `pose runtime run [--envelope] [--timeout <d>] <comando> [args...]` roda `review-check`, `closeout-check`, `artifact-check`, `surface-check` ou `roadmap-check` no runtime do worktree quando há um rodando, compatível e disposto, e standalone neste processo caso contrário; o código de saída e a saída são os do comando, e `--envelope` imprime um documento (`pose.runtime.run/1`) com eles e com `via` (`runtime` ou `standalone`), o `fallback_reason`, o `outcome` tipado (`completed`; `error` quando o comando rodou e terminou mas a saída (acima de 2 MiB) não volta, com o código de saída do comando; ou `unknown` para um prazo ou uma resposta perdida), a época e os acertos do memo. O runtime recusa, antes de o comando começar, o que ele não hospeda e um chamador cujo digest de ambiente (as variáveis `POSE_`, `HARNE8_`, `GIT_`, `CLAUDE` e `CODEX`, `XDG_STATE_HOME`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` e `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, o locale, `HOME`, `NO_COLOR` e o usuário) difere do dele, e o chamador roda standalone; um comando que chegou ao runtime e cuja resposta não voltou inteira, ou cujo `--timeout` passou, pode ter rodado e nunca é repetido standalone sem pedido: sai com 1 e diz isso. O runtime roda os comandos hospedados como o usuário que o iniciou, no seu worktree, com a saída de um não terminal, nas faixas de admissão descritas adiante; um comando que ainda espera o seu slot quando o `--timeout` passa é recusado, não iniciado, de modo que o chamador o roda standalone. Um runtime também guarda, entre comandos, a saída das leituras do Git que são funções puras de ids de objeto ou do commit que o HEAD nomeava quando rodaram, chaveada por esse commit e pelo estado raso (256 MiB, a menos recentemente usada primeiro; não para um worktree vinculado): o segundo `review-check` deste repositório levou cerca de 10 s contra cerca de 25 s standalone, com a mesma saída. O runtime admite os comandos hospedados em duas faixas, cada uma com seus slots e uma fila limitada: `short` (`surface-check`, cerca de 10 ms neste repositório) e `heavy` (os demais comandos hospedados; `roadmap-check <slug>` leva minutos aqui), 2 e 1 slots por padrão e filas de 16 e 4; `pose runtime start|serve` aceitam `--short-slots`, `--heavy-slots`, `--short-queue`, `--heavy-queue` e `--memo-mib` (padrão 256). Uma consulta curta não espera atrás de um check pesado, e um comando que encontra todos os slots da sua faixa ocupados e a fila cheia é recusado antes de esperar e roda standalone. `status` e o handshake trazem, por faixa, `<faixa>_slots`, `_queue_limit`, `_queued`, `_running`, `_admitted`, `_refused`, `_wait_ms_total` e `_wait_ms_max` em `metrics`. Uma medição do runtime num repositório real, com os limiares fixados antes dos números, é `POSE_RUNTIME_MEASURE=<arquivo> go test ./internal/cli -run TestMeasureTheRuntimeOnTheRealRepository`; o relatório fica em `.pose/reports/`.\\n\\n**Rodadas de review.** As rodadas de um escopo são os bundles selados em que uma review delegada concluída terminou, na ordem em que esses runs terminaram; `pose review verify`, `pose delegate ls` e `pose insights` mostram a rodada. A partir da rodada 2, o brief gerado (template `review-brief/2`; runs com `review-brief/1` continuam verificando) traz uma seção `Previous round` construída do ledger: cada finding aberto com o identificador que mantém entre rodadas (`R<rodada>-<id>`), sua severidade, disposição e local, o run anterior e o digest do seu draft. O draft do revisor é o bloco de código cercado cuja info string é `pose-draft`: JSON com `schema` `pose.review.draft/v1`, `decision` e `findings` (`id`, `severity` high|medium|low, `disposition` blocker|follow-up, `file`, `line`, `summary`), e a partir da rodada 2 `prior_findings` (`id`, `status` fixed|partial|not-fixed|superseded, `evidence`) e `sweep`. Um finding reportado `partial` ou `not-fixed` mantém seu identificador no brief seguinte. Um draft da rodada 2 em diante que deixa um finding aberto sem conta, marca um como `fixed` sem evidência ou não declara a varredura é registrado com `draft_problems`, o `wait` o reporta como `invalid`, e ele nunca é aceito como aprovação. Com `routing.rounds` `continue-thread` (o padrão), uma rodada retoma a sessão com que a rodada anterior terminou, pelo comando de resume do agente; ela abre uma sessão nova, registrando o motivo, quando o agente ou seu vendor muda, a cadeia de drafts está quebrada, essa sessão não pode ser retomada, ou a rodada atinge `routing.round_threshold` (5 por padrão), onde o `verify` também avisa que as rodadas não estão convergindo. `cold-session` abre uma sessão nova a cada rodada. Cada run registra sua `round` e sua `thread`; `pose insights` reporta, por escopo, findings por rodada, rodadas até a aprovação, e como os findings anteriores foram contabilizados em rodadas continuadas e frias. Uma rodada anterior cujo registro não está íntegro faz o brief recusar.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:1075:**Limites aceitos.** Uma spec pode declarar os limites residuais das suas garantias em `### Accepted limits`, um `- L<N>: <suposição>. Accepted because <motivo>.` por limite; `lint-spec --ready-check` recusa um limite que não diz por que é aceito e, quando um ADR cita a spec, um que o ADR não nomeia. O plano de review e o bundle selam os limites (`accepted_limits`, parte do digest do plano; num escopo com várias specs cada id é qualificado como `<spec>#L<N>`), e o brief do template `review-brief/11` os lista: um achado que cai dentro de um deles é um follow-up com `limit` igual ao seu id, registrado na atestação com a disposição `within-limit`, que não bloqueia uma aprovação seja qual for a severidade; um limite que o bundle não selou, ou um achado `within-limit` sem motivo, é recusado. Um revisor que ache um limite injustificado relata um achado que o contesta, que bloqueia como qualquer achado. Uma review converge classificando o que foi declarado fora do escopo, não por um teto de rodadas.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:1079:**Requests de review delegada.** `pose review request <escopo|bundle> --kind delegated-review [--purpose review|adjudicate|smoke] [--agents <nome,...>] [--note-file <caminho>] [--apply]` abre uma action request de kind `delegated-review` que vincula o projeto, o worktree, o escopo, o bundle selado, o brief, as notas, as policies, os agentes aceitos e o destino da conclusão (uma atestação, um registro de adjudicação ou evidência de smoke). Nenhum ator a responde: `pose action fulfil <act-id> --run <run-id> --runner agent:<id> --idempotency-key <chave> --expected-revision <n> [--channel cli|ci|conductor] [--envelope <arquivo>] [--apply]`, ou a tool MCP `pose_action_fulfil`, submete um run concluído, e o motor registra o fulfillment só quando o run é do brief e do bundle do request, de um agente aceito, concluído com sua contenção, sua prova assinada verifica, seu ledger de tentativas está íntegro e o motor registrou sua conclusão no destino do request; o fulfillment é verificado de novo a cada leitura. `pose delegate review <escopo> --request <act-id>` roda o brief do request localmente e submete o run depois de concluído. Um run que rejeita cumpre o request sem aprovar nada; um bundle superado ou uma policy alterada o invalida, mesmo já cumprido, e ele é pedido de novo com `--supersedes <act-id>`, que o aposenta sob as regras de autoridade de supersession (`--authority <json>`, `--role <papel>`, `--requested-at` do preview) ou, quando o plano passa a reservar a review a uma pessoa, faz da escalação para a pessoa o seu sucessor; um retry com a mesma chave e o mesmo run não faz nada. Uma pessoa é chamada por exceção, como decision request ao papel maintainer: um plano que reserva a review a uma pessoa, um orçamento de tentativas esgotado, revisores em conflito e uma `business_decision` que um revisor levantou no draft. A prova de um run verifica em qualquer checkout do projeto: o worktree onde rodou fica registrado, não é exigido de quem lê, então um job de CI ou o Conductor cumpre o request com o mesmo envelope.\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:197:pose spec-transfer apply --plan <file> --digest <sha256> --authorize-project <id>...\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:198:pose spec-transfer resume --operation <id> --project <id> --authorize-project <id>...\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:261:pose runtime run [--envelope] [--timeout <d>] <review-check|closeout-check|artifact-check|surface-check|roadmap-check> [args...]  # runs the check in the runtime when there is one, else standalone\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:274:pose insights [...]                # alias of stats; includes delegated draft metrics\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:278:pose recurrence-effect [--register ...] [--json] [--fail-ineffective]\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:279:pose record-deployment --environment <env> --deployment-kind <kind> [...]\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:280:pose record-incident --environment <env> [...]\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:288:pose extension <install|list|remove|verify> [...]\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:292:pose report --task \"...\" [--outcome pass|fail|partial|skipped|unknown] [--since <ref>] [--git-stage] [...]\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:293:pose amend <slug> [...]            # record an append-only spec amendment\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:298:pose action <open|list|show|statement|resolve|cancel|waive|invalidate> ...  # open, read and resolve material requests to a person or an external system\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:324:pose report-limitation --title \"...\" --kind limitation|bug|suggestion [--body \"...\"] [--submit]\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:409:- **Docs governance** (spec `pose-docs-governance-contract`) — an opt-in, per-project contract for documentation, same mechanic as the capability assessment above: absent, every project stays valid everywhere. `pose docs-init [--profile library|service|cli|monorepo]` scaffolds a manifest declaring governed doc roots and, per doc, `path`/`doc_type` (Diátaxis `tutorial`/`howto`/`reference`/`explanation`, or a custom value)/`topics`/`owns`/`applies_to`/optional `review_after`. `pose docs-check [--json] [--explain <rule>]` runs seven deterministic, offline rules — a declared doc missing from disk, a file present but undeclared, missing minimal frontmatter (`title`/`doc_type`), a broken relative link, a broken typed reference (`spec:`/`adr:`/`knowledge:`/`doc:`/...), staleness (an entry's own review date, or the manifest's default review window counted from its last touching commit) and a security scan reusing the same deterministic unsafe-instruction/secret-shaped pattern check skills already run — each with a configurable severity (`error`/`warning`/`off`). `pose check --strict` incorporates `docs-check` when the manifest exists (opt-in by presence); only errors block, warnings surface without blocking. `pose docs-sync` synchronizes governance metadata. Tool MCP: `pose_docs_state`. Project-state gains an additive Docs section (manifest presence/profile/roots, declared/undeclared/stale/error/warning counts).\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:560:     - `pose report-limitation --title \"...\" --kind limitation|bug|suggestion [--submit]`\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:943:**Hosted checks and the resident Git memo.** `pose runtime run [--envelope] [--timeout <d>] <command> [args...]` runs `review-check`, `closeout-check`, `artifact-check`, `surface-check` or `roadmap-check` in the runtime of the worktree when one is running, compatible and willing, and standalone in this process otherwise; the exit code and the output are the command's, and `--envelope` prints one document (`pose.runtime.run/1`) with them and with `via` (`runtime` or `standalone`), the `fallback_reason`, the typed `outcome` (`completed`; `error` when the command ran and ended but its output (over 2 MiB) is not sent back, with the command's exit code; or `unknown` for a deadline or a lost answer), the epoch and the memo hits. The runtime refuses, before the command starts, one it does not host and a caller whose environment digest (the `POSE_`, `HARNE8_`, `GIT_`, `CLAUDE` and `CODEX` variables, `XDG_STATE_HOME`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` and `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, the locale, `HOME`, `NO_COLOR` and the user) differs from its own, so the caller runs standalone; a command that reached the runtime and whose answer did not come back whole, or whose `--timeout` passed, may have run and is never repeated standalone unasked: it exits 1 and says so. The runtime runs hosted commands as the user that started it, in its worktree, with the output of a non-terminal, in the admission lanes described below; a command still waiting for its slot when its `--timeout` passes is refused, not started, so the caller runs it standalone. A runtime also keeps, between commands, the output of the Git reads that are pure functions of object ids or of the commit HEAD named when they ran, keyed by that commit and the shallow state (256 MiB, least recently used first; not for a linked worktree): the second `review-check` of this repository took about 10 s against about 25 s standalone, with the same output. The runtime admits hosted commands in two lanes, each with its own slots and a bounded queue: `short` (`surface-check`, about 10 ms on this repository) and `heavy` (the other hosted commands; `roadmap-check <slug>` takes minutes here), 2 and 1 slots by default and queues of 16 and 4; `pose runtime start|serve` take `--short-slots`, `--heavy-slots`, `--short-queue`, `--heavy-queue` and `--memo-mib` (default 256). A short query is not queued behind a heavy check, and a command that finds every slot of its lane busy and the queue full is refused before it waits and runs standalone. `status` and the handshake carry, per lane, `<lane>_slots`, `_queue_limit`, `_queued`, `_running`, `_admitted`, `_refused`, `_wait_ms_total` and `_wait_ms_max` in `metrics`. A measurement of the runtime on a real repository, with its thresholds fixed before the numbers, is `POSE_RUNTIME_MEASURE=<file> go test ./internal/cli -run TestMeasureTheRuntimeOnTheRealRepository`; its report is under `.pose/reports/`.\\n\\n**Review rounds.** The rounds of a scope are its sealed bundles that a completed delegated review concluded on, in the order those runs ended; `pose review verify`, `pose delegate ls` and `pose insights` show the round. From round 2, the generated brief (template `review-brief/2`; runs briefed with `review-brief/1` still verify) carries a `Previous round` section built from the ledger: every open finding with the identifier it keeps across rounds (`R<round>-<id>`), its severity, disposition and location, the previous run and its draft digest. The reviewer's draft is the fenced code block whose info string is `pose-draft`: JSON with `schema` `pose.review.draft/v1`, `decision` and `findings` (`id`, `severity` high|medium|low, `disposition` blocker|follow-up, `file`, `line`, `summary`), and from round 2 `prior_findings` (`id`, `status` fixed|partial|not-fixed|superseded, `evidence`) and `sweep`. A finding reported `partial` or `not-fixed` keeps its identifier in the next brief. A round-2-or-later draft that leaves an open finding unaccounted, calls one `fixed` without evidence or states no sweep is recorded with `draft_problems`, `wait` reports it as `invalid`, and it is never accepted as an approval. With `routing.rounds` `continue-thread` (the default) a round resumes the session the previous round ended with, through the agent's resume command; it opens a new session, recording why, when the agent or its vendor changes, the chain of drafts is broken, that session cannot be resumed, or the round reaches `routing.round_threshold` (5 by default), where `verify` also warns that the rounds are not converging. `cold-session` opens a new session every round. Each run records its `round` and `thread`; `pose insights` reports, per scope, findings per round, rounds to approval, and how prior findings were accounted for in continued and in cold rounds. A previous round whose record is not intact makes the brief refuse.\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:1065:**Accepted limits.** A spec may declare the residual limits of its guarantees under `### Accepted limits`, one `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` per limit; `lint-spec --ready-check` refuses a limit that does not say why it is accepted, and, when an ADR cites the spec, one the ADR does not name. The review plan and the bundle seal the limits (`accepted_limits`, part of the plan digest; in a scope with several specs each id is qualified as `<spec>#L<N>`), and the brief of template `review-brief/11` lists them: a finding that falls inside one is a follow-up with `limit` set to its id, recorded in the attestation with disposition `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity; a limit the bundle did not seal, or a `within-limit` finding without a reason, is refused. A reviewer who finds a limit unjustified reports a finding that disputes it, which blocks as any finding does. A review converges by classifying what was declared out of scope, not by a cap on rounds.\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:1069:**Delegated-review requests.** `pose review request <scope|bundle> --kind delegated-review [--purpose review|adjudicate|smoke] [--agents <name,...>] [--note-file <path>] [--apply]` opens an action request of kind `delegated-review` that binds the project, the worktree, the scope, the sealed bundle, the brief, the notes, the policies, the accepted agents and the destination of the conclusion (an attestation, an adjudication record or smoke evidence). No actor answers it: `pose action fulfil <act-id> --run <run-id> --runner agent:<id> --idempotency-key <key> --expected-revision <n> [--channel cli|ci|conductor] [--envelope <file>] [--apply]`, or the MCP tool `pose_action_fulfil`, submits a completed run, and the engine records the fulfilment only when the run is of the request's brief and bundle, of an accepted agent, completed with its containment, its signed proof verifies, its attempt ledger is intact and the engine recorded its conclusion at the request's destination; the fulfilment is verified again on every read. `pose delegate review <scope> --request <act-id>` runs the request's brief locally and submits the run once concluded. A rejecting run fulfils the request without approving anything; a superseded bundle or changed policy invalidates it, even once fulfilled, and it is asked again with `--supersedes <act-id>`, which retires it under the supersession authority rules (`--authority <json>`, `--role <role>`, `--requested-at` from the preview), or, when the plan now reserves the review for a person, makes the person's escalation its successor; a retry with the same key and run is a no-op. A person is asked by exception, as a decision request to the maintainer role: a plan that reserves the review for a person, a spent attempt budget, reviewers in conflict, and a `business_decision` a reviewer raised in its draft. A run's proof verifies in any checkout of the project: the worktree it ran in is recorded, not required of the reader, so a CI job or the Conductor fulfils the request with the same envelope.\nPOSE.md:197:pose spec-transfer apply --plan <file> --digest <sha256> --authorize-project <id>...\nPOSE.md:198:pose spec-transfer resume --operation <id> --project <id> --authorize-project <id>...\nPOSE.md:261:pose runtime run [--envelope] [--timeout <d>] <review-check|closeout-check|artifact-check|surface-check|roadmap-check> [args...]  # runs the check in the runtime when there is one, else standalone\nPOSE.md:274:pose insights [...]                # alias of stats; includes delegated draft metrics\nPOSE.md:278:pose recurrence-effect [--register ...] [--json] [--fail-ineffective]\nPOSE.md:279:pose record-deployment --environment <env> --deployment-kind <kind> [...]\nPOSE.md:280:pose record-incident --environment <env> [...]\nPOSE.md:288:pose extension <install|list|remove|verify> [...]\nPOSE.md:292:pose report --task \"...\" [--outcome pass|fail|partial|skipped|unknown] [--since <ref>] [--git-stage] [...]\nPOSE.md:293:pose amend <slug> [...]            # record an append-only spec amendment\nPOSE.md:298:pose action <open|list|show|statement|resolve|cancel|waive|invalidate> ...  # open, read and resolve material requests to a person or an external system\nPOSE.md:324:pose report-limitation --title \"...\" --kind limitation|bug|suggestion [--body \"...\"] [--submit]\nPOSE.md:409:- **Docs governance** (spec `pose-docs-governance-contract`) — an opt-in, per-project contract for documentation, same mechanic as the capability assessment above: absent, every project stays valid everywhere. `pose docs-init [--profile library|service|cli|monorepo]` scaffolds a manifest declaring governed doc roots and, per doc, `path`/`doc_type` (Diátaxis `tutorial`/`howto`/`reference`/`explanation`, or a custom value)/`topics`/`owns`/`applies_to`/optional `review_after`. `pose docs-check [--json] [--explain <rule>]` runs seven deterministic, offline rules — a declared doc missing from disk, a file present but undeclared, missing minimal frontmatter (`title`/`doc_type`), a broken relative link, a broken typed reference (`spec:`/`adr:`/`knowledge:`/`doc:`/...), staleness (an entry's own review date, or the manifest's default review window counted from its last touching commit) and a security scan reusing the same deterministic unsafe-instruction/secret-shaped pattern check skills already run — each with a configurable severity (`error`/`warning`/`off`). `pose check --strict` incorporates `docs-check` when the manifest exists (opt-in by presence); only errors block, warnings surface without blocking. `pose docs-sync` synchronizes governance metadata. Tool MCP: `pose_docs_state`. Project-state gains an additive Docs section (manifest presence/profile/roots, declared/undeclared/stale/error/warning counts).\nPOSE.md:560:     - `pose report-limitation --title \"...\" --kind limitation|bug|suggestion [--submit]`\nPOSE.md:956:**Hosted checks and the resident Git memo.** `pose runtime run [--envelope] [--timeout <d>] <command> [args...]` runs `review-check`, `closeout-check`, `artifact-check`, `surface-check` or `roadmap-check` in the runtime of the worktree when one is running, compatible and willing, and standalone in this process otherwise; the exit code and the output are the command's, and `--envelope` prints one document (`pose.runtime.run/1`) with them and with `via` (`runtime` or `standalone`), the `fallback_reason`, the typed `outcome` (`completed`; `error` when the command ran and ended but its output (over 2 MiB) is not sent back, with the command's exit code; or `unknown` for a deadline or a lost answer), the epoch and the memo hits. The runtime refuses, before the command starts, one it does not host and a caller whose environment digest (the `POSE_`, `HARNE8_`, `GIT_`, `CLAUDE` and `CODEX` variables, `XDG_STATE_HOME`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` and `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, the locale, `HOME`, `NO_COLOR` and the user) differs from its own, so the caller runs standalone; a command that reached the runtime and whose answer did not come back whole, or whose `--timeout` passed, may have run and is never repeated standalone unasked: it exits 1 and says so. The runtime runs hosted commands as the user that started it, in its worktree, with the output of a non-terminal, in the admission lanes described below; a command still waiting for its slot when its `--timeout` passes is refused, not started, so the caller runs it standalone. A runtime also keeps, between commands, the output of the Git reads that are pure functions of object ids or of the commit HEAD named when they ran, keyed by that commit and the shallow state (256 MiB, least recently used first; not for a linked worktree): the second `review-check` of this repository took about 10 s against about 25 s standalone, with the same output. The runtime admits hosted commands in two lanes, each with its own slots and a bounded queue: `short` (`surface-check`, about 10 ms on this repository) and `heavy` (the other hosted commands; `roadmap-check <slug>` takes minutes here), 2 and 1 slots by default and queues of 16 and 4; `pose runtime start|serve` take `--short-slots`, `--heavy-slots`, `--short-queue`, `--heavy-queue` and `--memo-mib` (default 256). A short query is not queued behind a heavy check, and a command that finds every slot of its lane busy and the queue full is refused before it waits and runs standalone. `status` and the handshake carry, per lane, `<lane>_slots`, `_queue_limit`, `_queued`, `_running`, `_admitted`, `_refused`, `_wait_ms_total` and `_wait_ms_max` in `metrics`. A measurement of the runtime on a real repository, with its thresholds fixed before the numbers, is `POSE_RUNTIME_MEASURE=<file> go test ./internal/cli -run TestMeasureTheRuntimeOnTheRealRepository`; its report is under `.pose/reports/`.\\n\\n**Review rounds.** The rounds of a scope are its sealed bundles that a completed delegated review concluded on, in the order those runs ended; `pose review verify`, `pose delegate ls` and `pose insights` show the round. From round 2, the generated brief (template `review-brief/2`; runs briefed with `review-brief/1` still verify) carries a `Previous round` section built from the ledger: every open finding with the identifier it keeps across rounds (`R<round>-<id>`), its severity, disposition and location, the previous run and its draft digest. The reviewer's draft is the fenced code block whose info string is `pose-draft`: JSON with `schema` `pose.review.draft/v1`, `decision` and `findings` (`id`, `severity` high|medium|low, `disposition` blocker|follow-up, `file`, `line`, `summary`), and from round 2 `prior_findings` (`id`, `status` fixed|partial|not-fixed|superseded, `evidence`) and `sweep`. A finding reported `partial` or `not-fixed` keeps its identifier in the next brief. A round-2-or-later draft that leaves an open finding unaccounted, calls one `fixed` without evidence or states no sweep is recorded with `draft_problems`, `wait` reports it as `invalid`, and it is never accepted as an approval. With `routing.rounds` `continue-thread` (the default) a round resumes the session the previous round ended with, through the agent's resume command; it opens a new session, recording why, when the agent or its vendor changes, the chain of drafts is broken, that session cannot be resumed, or the round reaches `routing.round_threshold` (5 by default), where `verify` also warns that the rounds are not converging. `cold-session` opens a new session every round. Each run records its `round` and `thread`; `pose insights` reports, per scope, findings per round, rounds to approval, and how prior findings were accounted for in continued and in cold rounds. A previous round whose record is not intact makes the brief refuse.\nPOSE.md:1078:**Accepted limits.** A spec may declare the residual limits of its guarantees under `### Accepted limits`, one `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` per limit; `lint-spec --ready-check` refuses a limit that does not say why it is accepted, and, when an ADR cites the spec, one the ADR does not name. The review plan and the bundle seal the limits (`accepted_limits`, part of the plan digest; in a scope with several specs each id is qualified as `<spec>#L<N>`), and the brief of template `review-brief/11` lists them: a finding that falls inside one is a follow-up with `limit` set to its id, recorded in the attestation with disposition `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity; a limit the bundle did not seal, or a `within-limit` finding without a reason, is refused. A reviewer who finds a limit unjustified reports a finding that disputes it, which blocks as any finding does. A review converges by classifying what was declared out of scope, not by a cap on rounds.\nPOSE.md:1082:**Delegated-review requests.** `pose review request <scope|bundle> --kind delegated-review [--purpose review|adjudicate|smoke] [--agents <name,...>] [--note-file <path>] [--apply]` opens an action request of kind `delegated-review` that binds the project, the worktree, the scope, the sealed bundle, the brief, the notes, the policies, the accepted agents and the destination of the conclusion (an attestation, an adjudication record or smoke evidence). No actor answers it: `pose action fulfil <act-id> --run <run-id> --runner agent:<id> --idempotency-key <key> --expected-revision <n> [--channel cli|ci|conductor] [--envelope <file>] [--apply]`, or the MCP tool `pose_action_fulfil`, submits a completed run, and the engine records the fulfilment only when the run is of the request's brief and bundle, of an accepted agent, completed with its containment, its signed proof verifies, its attempt ledger is intact and the engine recorded its conclusion at the request's destination; the fulfilment is verified again on every read. `pose delegate review <scope> --request <act-id>` runs the request's brief locally and submits the run once concluded. A rejecting run fulfils the request without approving anything; a superseded bundle or changed policy invalidates it, even once fulfilled, and it is asked again with `--supersedes <act-id>`, which retires it under the supersession authority rules (`--authority <json>`, `--role <role>`, `--requested-at` from the preview), or, when the plan now reserves the review for a person, makes the person's escalation its successor; a retry with the same key and run is a no-op. A person is asked by exception, as a decision request to the maintainer role: a plan that reserves the review for a person, a spent attempt budget, reviewers in conflict, and a `business_decision` a reviewer raised in its draft. A run's proof verifies in any checkout of the project: the worktree it ran in is recorded, not required of the reader, so a CI job or the Conductor fulfils the request with the same envelope.\npose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go:433:func lintOneSpec(specPath string, requiredOnly, readyCheck bool, stdout, stderr io.Writer, designCheck ...bool) int {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go:559:\t\t\tlint.finding(cliout.StateError, \"dor\", cliText(locale, \"DoR: no acceptance criterion has a stable ID (use '- R<N>: ...' bullets in Requirements)\", \"DoR: nenhum acceptance criterion com ID estável (use bullets '- R<N>: ...' em Requirements)\"))\npose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go:573:\t\tlimits, limitProblems := posepkg.ParseAcceptedLimits(text)\npose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go:603:\ttargets := append([]string{}, required...)\npose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go:605:\t\ttargets = append(targets, optionalSections...)\npose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go:1217:\t\t\tmissing = append(missing, \"DoR: accepted limit \"+l.ID+\" is not named by the ADR that cites \"+slug)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
